summary: MLS Policy Investigation Plan
description: |
    The initial problem was thought to be an infrastructure limitation
    preventing MLS policy testing. However, experimentation has shown that
    the issue lies within the selinux-policy itself, specifically with
    missing rules for cloud-init in the MLS policy.

    A candidate fix lives on JJanasek/selinux-policy@mls-enable-cloud-init
    (https://github.com/JJanasek/selinux-policy/pull/new/mls-enable-cloud-init):
    it enables the cloudform module for the mls policy store. Since
    cloudform doesn't exist in the stock mls store at all yet, this plan
    builds it as a standalone third-party module with the standard
    `/usr/share/selinux/devel/Makefile` workflow (the same one
    `policycoreutils-devel` documents for building a single module against
    whatever policy is actually installed) and adds it to the guest's live
    mls store with `semodule -i`, before relabeling/rebooting, so we can
    confirm the fix clears the cloud-init AVC denials. The module's source
    files (cloudform.te/if/fc plus the new NetworkManager and ssh
    interfaces) are checked into this plan under mls-cloudinit-fix/,
    mirroring the fork commits, so the build has no runtime dependency on
    GitHub.

    The first pass of this fix only added NetworkManager/net_conf rules
    and got cloud-init transitioning into cloud_init_t for the first time
    (previously it silently ran unconfined as init_t, because cloudform.te
    relies on `unconfined_domain(cloud_init_t)` for targeted/mcs, which
    isn't enabled under mls). That surfaced a much larger set of small,
    previously-invisible gaps in cloudform.te's own ruleset -- dhcpcd pid/
    signal handling, systemd unit-file status queries, udev trigger/settle,
    ssh host key reads and ~/.ssh management, shadow reads for the
    set-passwords module, and locale/timezone file management -- which
    the second pass adds. A third pass, after re-running with only the
    prior two passes' rules and diffing the fresh denial set, closes three
    more boot-to-boot-variable gaps that hadn't shown up in every run:
    cloud-init's own stage-synchronization sockets under
    /run/cloud-init/share/*.sock are a distinct sock_file object class
    from the plain files sysnet_manage_config() already covers (needs its
    own sysnet_manage_config_sockets() interface), `setgid` (dropping to
    the `adm` group before writing command-output logs) is a distinct
    capability from the ones already granted, and `systemd-detect-virt`'s
    container check needs nsfs file getattr.

    `storage_raw_read_fixed_disk()` hitting a raw block device labeled
    s15 while cloud_init_t runs at s0 turned out to be a genuine MLS BLP
    "no read up" constraint (`mlsconstrain`), not a missing TE rule --
    `audit2allow`'s suggested `allow` for it is a no-op, since the
    constraint check is independent of and in addition to normal TE
    rules. The fourth pass makes the deliberate call to fix this: growpart
    (cc_growpart.py, run in cloud_init_t itself since it has no dedicated
    domain) legitimately needs to read the raw disk to grow the root
    filesystem regardless of its labeled sensitivity, so cloud_init_t is
    declared MLS-trusted for file reads at all levels via
    `mls_file_read_all_levels()` -- the first domain in the tree to use
    that attribute.

    Tried switching to real enforcing mode to test acceptance criterion
    1 directly, expecting the earlier unlabeled_t denials to be a
    transient relabel-vs-early-boot race that a *second* reboot (without
    touching /.autorelabel again, so no relabel runs the second time)
    would dodge. It didn't: the second, already-fully-relabeled boot hit
    the exact same /etc/ld.so.cache denial, now enforced for real, which
    cascades into udev helpers failing to exec (can't map their own
    ld.so.cache) -> disk-by-uuid symlinks never appearing -> local-fs
    mount timeouts -> emergency mode -- and emergency mode is a dead end
    too, since root is locked, so the guest hangs forever and tmt's
    reconnect times out. So this was never a race; the file's on-disk
    context is durably wrong (`unconfined_u:object_r:ld_so_cache_t:s0`
    -- a leftover *targeted*-policy context; unconfined_u isn't even a
    valid mls user).

    Root-caused it: plain restorecon/setfiles (as run by the standard
    boot-time `/.autorelabel` relabel) only fix a file's *type*, never
    its user/role/range, unless given `-F`. Since `ld_so_cache_t` is the
    same type under both targeted and mls, the relabel sees "type
    already correct" and leaves the stale targeted-policy user in
    place forever -- confirmed directly: plain `restorecon` reports no
    change, `restorecon -F` fixes it (`unconfined_u` -> `system_u`).
    `prepare_for_mls.sh` now does a forced, full-context
    `restorecon -RF /` after the reboot (`prepare_for_mls_force_relabel`)
    to fix this for every file, not just ld.so.cache -- necessary since
    installing/configuring anything before the reboot (which is
    unavoidable) leaves files with the old policy's user on disk.

prepare:
  - name: Install and configure MLS policy in Permissive mode
    how: shell
    script: |
      dnf install -y selinux-policy-mls policycoreutils-python-utils audit setools-console

      # prepare_for_mls_configure() (tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh) does the
      # correct, minimal, documented setup for switching to mls: mapping
      # root to sysadm_u, pointing SELINUXTYPE at the mls store, and
      # scheduling a full relabel. It's split from prepare_for_mls_reboot()
      # here because the next prepare step below needs to stage the
      # cloudform module fix into the (still inactive) mls store before we
      # actually reboot into it.
      source $TMT_TREE/tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh
      prepare_for_mls_configure

      # IMPORTANT: Switch to permissive to survive the boot relabel.
      # Permissive still logs every AVC that would have been denied, so
      # this is sufficient for capturing denials without risking another
      # boot hang from a real enforcing-mode block. This is a workaround
      # for still-open bugs (see the relabel-race denials this plan
      # captures below), not part of the general/correct MLS switch
      # procedure documented in prepare_for_mls.sh, so it stays here
      # instead of in that shared library.
      sed -i 's/^SELINUX=.*/SELINUX=permissive/' /etc/selinux/config

      # IMPORTANT: cloud-init's `ssh` module deletes and regenerates the
      # SSH host keys on what it thinks is a fresh instance boot (see
      # below). If the host key changes, tmt/Testing Farm's SSH connection
      # back to this guest can fail in a way that just looks like "still
      # waiting for the guest to come back", hanging well past the reboot
      # timeout instead of failing cleanly. Tell cloud-init to keep the
      # existing keys instead of deleting them.
      mkdir -p /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d
      echo 'ssh_deletekeys: false' > /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-preserve-ssh-host-keys.cfg

      # Reset cloud-init's per-instance state so that on the *next* boot
      # (triggered below) systemd re-runs the full local -> init -> config
      # -> final stage sequence for real, under the correct SELinux domain
      # transitions. Manually re-invoking `cloud-init` from a login shell
      # after boot does NOT reproduce this: it skips the config/final
      # stages (most filesystem-changing modules live there) and it runs
      # in your login domain instead of the dedicated cloud-init domain(s),
      # since the domain transition only happens when systemd starts the
      # units.
      cloud-init clean --logs

  - name: Build and load cloud-init policy fix (JJanasek/selinux-policy@mls-enable-cloud-init)
    how: shell
    script: |
      dnf install -y policycoreutils-devel

      # IMPORTANT: don't rebuild the whole mls base + module set from
      # source (tried first; see git history of this file for why that
      # path -- and hot-patching individual modules from it into the stock
      # store -- both failed). cloudform doesn't exist in the installed mls
      # store at all yet, so adding it is a pure `semodule -i` of a new
      # module, not a replacement of anything -- there's no version to
      # reconcile. The standard, supported way to build *one* module like
      # this against whatever policy is actually installed is the devel
      # Makefile shipped by policycoreutils-devel/selinux-policy-devel: it
      # builds against /usr/share/selinux/devel/include, the same
      # interface headers (corenetwork.if, sysnet.if, ...) already backing
      # the live store, so there's no cross-snapshot mismatch to hit.
      #
      # cloudform.te calls our new networkmanager_manage_rw_conf,
      # ssh_read_server_keys, and sysnet_manage_config_sockets interfaces,
      # which only exist on the mls-enable-cloud-init fork's
      # networkmanager.if/ssh.if/sysnetwork.if, not in the stock headers.
      # All three interfaces just expand into ordinary allow/manage_*_pattern
      # rules against types the already-installed networkmanager/ssh/
      # sysnetwork modules provide -- so we don't need to rebuild or
      # reinstall those modules at all. The devel Makefile picks up every
      # *.if file under each header layer directory, so dropping our new
      # interfaces in as their own files alongside the stock
      # networkmanager.if/ssh.if/sysnetwork.if is enough; nothing installed
      # needs to be touched or replaced.
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/networkmanager-manage-rw-conf.if \
          /usr/share/selinux/devel/include/contrib/
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/ssh-read-server-keys.if \
          /usr/share/selinux/devel/include/contrib/
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/sysnetwork-manage-config-sockets.if \
          /usr/share/selinux/devel/include/system/

      mkdir -p /root/build && cd /root/build
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/cloudform.te .
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/cloudform.if .
      cp $TMT_TREE/tmt/mls-cloudinit-fix/cloudform.fc .

      make -f /usr/share/selinux/devel/Makefile cloudform.pp

      # IMPORTANT: -n / --noreload. `semodule -i` operates on the store
      # named by /etc/selinux/config's SELINUXTYPE -- which we already set
      # to "mls" in the previous prepare step -- but by *default* it also
      # pushes that store live into the running kernel right away via
      # security_load_policy(), regardless of whether that store matches
      # the policy the kernel is actually running. We're still running the
      # guest's original (targeted) boot policy at this point (the mls
      # store won't actually become the *active* kernel policy until the
      # reboot below, via /.autorelabel), so forcing the just-built mls
      # store live now would instantly apply MLS-level checks to every
      # already-running process/file still labeled under targeted contexts
      # -- which is exactly what happened the first time this was tried
      # without -n: the guest broke immediately (`/root/.bashrc: Permission
      # denied`, tmt's own log-pulling rsync failing with EACCES, ...)
      # because the live kernel was suddenly enforcing MLS semantics no
      # running context actually satisfied. -n keeps this an entirely
      # offline edit of the (currently inactive) mls store.
      semodule -n -i cloudform.pp
      semodule -l | grep '^cloudform\b'

  - name: Reboot to apply MLS and Relabel
    how: shell
    script: |
      # prepare_for_mls_reboot() (tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh) already guards
      # against tmt re-running this step from the top on every reconnect
      # (which, unguarded, requested a fresh reboot every time and never
      # converged) via TMT_REBOOT_COUNT.
      source $TMT_TREE/tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh
      prepare_for_mls_reboot

  - name: Confirm and fix the /etc/ld.so.cache invalid post-relabel context
    how: shell
    script: |
      # A second, already-fully-relabeled reboot into enforcing still hit
      # the exact same /etc/ld.so.cache denial as the very first boot --
      # so this was never a transient relabel-vs-early-boot *race* (which
      # would have cleared by the second boot); the file's on-disk
      # context is durably wrong. Captured trawcon on the enforcing run:
      #   unconfined_u:object_r:ld_so_cache_t:s0
      # unconfined_u isn't even a valid SELinux user under mls, and s0
      # alone isn't a valid mls range for a system object -- this reads
      # exactly like a leftover *targeted*-policy context that a full
      # mls relabel should have overwritten, but didn't.
      #
      # Confirmed root cause: restorecon/setfiles only fix the *type*
      # component of a context by default; fixing the user/role/range
      # too requires -F. ld_so_cache_t is the same type name under both
      # targeted and mls, so the boot-time relabel sees "type already
      # correct" and never touches the stale unconfined_u/s0 left over
      # from before the policy switch (matchpathcon -V even reports the
      # file as "verified" for the same reason). Demonstrated below, then
      # fixed properly for real via prepare_for_mls_force_relabel()
      # (tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh), which now does a forced, full-context
      # `restorecon -RF /` after the reboot -- see its comment for why
      # this can't run before the reboot.
      echo "=== current on-disk context ==="
      ls -Z /etc/ld.so.cache
      stat /etc/ld.so.cache

      echo "=== what mls file_contexts says this path should be (full context) ==="
      matchpathcon -V /etc/ld.so.cache

      echo "=== does a normal (type-only) restorecon think there's anything to fix? ==="
      restorecon -v /etc/ld.so.cache; echo "exit=$?"
      ls -Z /etc/ld.so.cache

      echo "=== full-context relabel of the whole filesystem (the actual fix) ==="
      source $TMT_TREE/tmt/prepare_for_mls.sh
      prepare_for_mls_force_relabel

      echo "=== /etc/ld.so.cache context after the forced relabel ==="
      ls -Z /etc/ld.so.cache

  - name: Switch to Enforcing and reboot again (tests acceptance criterion 1 for real)
    how: shell
    script: |
      # Gated behind TEST_ENFORCING (default on now that the previous
      # step's forced relabel should have fixed the root cause of the
      # hang this step used to reproduce -- flip it off again if it
      # regresses without wanting to burn 20 minutes on every run).
      if [ "${TEST_ENFORCING:-1}" = "1" ] && [ ! -f /root/.mls-enforcing-switch-done ]; then
          touch /root/.mls-enforcing-switch-done

          # The ld.so.cache fix above cleared that denial, but the very
          # next enforcing boot then hung on a *different*, pre-existing
          # gap in the stock mls policy's systemd module -- unrelated to
          # cloudform/cloud-init, and with a long history of similar
          # one-off bugs upstream (e.g. RHEL-14289, which had to add a
          # private semanage_exec_t type just so selinux-autorelabel.service
          # itself could run under mls):
          #   - systemd_selinux_autorelabel_generator_t denied `execute`
          #     on shell_exec_t (can't even exec bash)
          #   - several *_generator_t domains (getty, sysv, factory-reset)
          #     denied `write` on kmsg_device_t:s15 -- a genuine mls BLP
          #     "no write up" constraint, since these domains run at the
          #     low end (s0) of their s0-s15 range by default
          # Mark just these specific domains permissive (logged, not
          # blocked) purely to see what -- if anything -- is left once
          # they're out of the way; this is a diagnostic probe, not a
          # real fix (a real fix belongs in selinux-policy's systemd
          # module, not here).
          semanage permissive -a systemd_selinux_autorelabel_generator_t
          semanage permissive -a systemd_factory_reset_generator_t
          semanage permissive -a systemd_getty_generator_t
          semanage permissive -a systemd_sysv_generator_t

          sed -i 's/^SELINUX=.*/SELINUX=enforcing/' /etc/selinux/config

          # Force a full, fresh local -> init -> config -> final cloud-init
          # run under enforcing on the next boot (see the "Reset cloud-init's
          # per-instance state" comment in the first prepare step -- without
          # this, cloud-init would just see the instance as already
          # provisioned and skip everything).
          cloud-init clean --logs

          tmt-reboot -t 1200
      fi

execute:
  how: tmt
  script: |
    echo "=== SELinux status ==="
    getenforce
    sestatus

    echo "=== cloud-init result from this boot ==="
    cloud-init status --long || true

    echo "=== cloud-init unit activity from this boot ==="
    journalctl -b -u 'cloud-init*' --no-pager || true

    # Earlier runs showed the main /usr/bin/cloud-init binary correctly
    # transitioning into cloud_init_t. But the cloud-init-generator systemd
    # generator (/usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator --
    # NOTE: earlier versions of this diagnostic had the wrong filename,
    # "cloud-init.local-generator", which never existed, so this check was
    # silently a no-op) is observed running as scontext=initrc_t instead of
    # cloud_init_t, even though cloudform.fc already matches it via the
    # same `cloud-init.*` pattern as the main binary. That process is also
    # seen hitting the already-known /etc/ld.so.cache unlabeled_t denial
    # in the very same boot -- suggesting this generator is just another
    # casualty of the same relabel-race bug (some files not getting their
    # real label applied before early-boot processes need them), rather
    # than a distinct selinux-policy .te gap. These checks confirm that:
    #   1) whether the generator's on-disk label actually matches what
    #      cloudform.fc expects (cloud_init_exec_t) or not, and
    #   2) whether a transition rule exists from *either* init_t or
    #      initrc_t (systemd may invoke generators from either, depending
    #      on version/config) to cloud_init_t, in case the label is fine
    #      but the transition is scoped to the wrong source domain.
    echo "=== cloud-init entrypoint labels: on-disk vs policy-expected ==="
    ls -Z /usr/bin/cloud-init /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator 2>&1 || true
    matchpathcon -V /usr/bin/cloud-init /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-preserve-ssh-host-keys.cfg /etc/ld.so.cache 2>&1 || true
    restorecon -nv /usr/bin/cloud-init /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/cloud-init-generator /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/99-preserve-ssh-host-keys.cfg /etc/ld.so.cache 2>&1 || true

    echo "=== does the loaded MLS policy define an init_t or initrc_t -> cloud_init_t transition? ==="
    sesearch -A -s init_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c file -p execute 2>&1 || true
    sesearch -T -s init_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c process 2>&1 || true
    sesearch -A -s initrc_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c file -p execute 2>&1 || true
    sesearch -T -s initrc_t -t cloud_init_exec_t -c process 2>&1 || true

    echo "=== actual domain cloud-init is running in (should be cloud_init_t) ==="
    ps -eZ | grep -i cloud || true

    echo "=== CAPTURED AVC DENIALS (this boot) ==="
    ausearch -m AVC,USER_AVC -ts boot || true

    echo "=== Suggested policy for observed denials ==="
    ausearch -m AVC -ts boot | audit2allow || true

    # Relabel-race investigation: does /.autorelabel even get consumed on
    # this image? systemd/dracut's autorelabel handling deletes the flag
    # file itself once the relabel completes -- if it's still present
    # after boot, the relabel service never ran at all, which would
    # explain the unlabeled_t denials far better than a race between
    # relabel and early boot units.
    echo "=== is /.autorelabel still present after boot? (it should have been consumed) ==="
    ls -la /.autorelabel 2>&1 || echo "/.autorelabel absent (consumed, as expected)"

    echo "=== does a selinux-autorelabel(-mark).service unit exist / did it run? ==="
    systemctl list-unit-files 'selinux-autorelabel*' 2>&1 || true
    systemctl status selinux-autorelabel.service 2>&1 || true
    journalctl -b -u 'selinux-autorelabel*' --no-pager -o short-monotonic 2>&1 || true

    echo "=== full early-boot unit timeline, for correlating relabel vs cloud-init start ==="
    journalctl -b --no-pager -o short-monotonic | head -200 || true

    echo "=== exact epoch timestamps: first unlabeled_t AVC vs cloud-init-local.service start ==="
    ausearch -m AVC -ts boot 2>/dev/null | grep -m1 unlabeled_t || true
    journalctl -b -u cloud-init-local.service --no-pager -o short-precise 2>&1 | head -5 || true
